            <ArticleTitle>Blood markers of inflammation and endothelial dysfunction in cardioembolic stroke: systematic review and meta-analysis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>200-209</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1080/1354750X.2017.1286689</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Various processes including inflammation and endothelial dysfunction have been implicated in the pathogenesis of cardioembolic (CE) strokes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To review the evidence and investigate the association between immune-inflammatory biomarkers and CE strokes versus other stroke subtypes.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">We systematically reviewed the literature (sources: MEDLINE, web-based register http://stroke-biomarkers.com , reference lists) with quality assessment and meta-analysis of selected articles.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The most consistent association was found between C-reactive protein (CRP) and CE strokes when compared to other stroke subtypes (standardized mean difference 0.223 (0.116, 0.343); p &lt; 0.001) Conclusions: Our findings confirm a possible association between selected inflammatory biomarkers and CE stroke.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
